## The Four Process Activities: RetailSync Case Study
Specification - weak
Development - present but weak
Validation - weak
Evolution - Entirely missing


Kick off - No specification was created, only a one hour meeting was had which only described 'roughly whta was needed'. A project cannot be built on a one hour meeting. A project has to be planned taking into consideration who is using the product, what are the requiremnts that the project has to meet to be considered successful, what tool are needed to complete this project, and who is going to carry out this project,
along with budget and time constraints. A project with no specification is already bound to fail.

Development - while development took place, the developers worked independently, just taking a module each without considering how each module my work with the other or affect it, there was no coordination and hence they often overwrote each others code. There were no shared coding standards which means there was bound to be confusion. No warehouse staff was consulted during development, which means developers were only guessing how the system was going to work.

Change of plan - it was only metioned in passing that the system would need to support the company's new second warehouse, a feat that should have been detailed in the intital specification and has only been communicated in passing 4 months into the project. The developers did not revisit the design but just patched on a new requirement on top of an exiating schema.

Testing - The developers ran the application themselves and fixed the the crashes they enocountered, no actual warehouse staff tested the system and no test cases were written so the developers have very little idea on if the system works as is intended to

Go Live - The problems that occured during this stage were an accumulation of the lack of project management that happened in the previous stages.

I believe the failure that did the most damage was the lack of proper specification. This caused the developers to create a project without having an idea how the system was supposed to work and not knowing what requirements were to be met. 

## Reseaching a Software Failure
On June 4, 1966, the Ariane 5 rocket exploded roughly 40 seconds after launch during it maiden flight. The failure was caused by a software crash in the Inertial Reference system. While executing data conversion, the software attempted to convert a 64 bit floating point number representing horizontal velocity into a 16 bit integer. Because Ariane 5 experienced higher accelration that its predecessor, the 64 bit value exceeded 32,767, the maximum limit storable in a 16 bit integer,
this resulted in an unhandled operand exception. The primary and backup SRI processors shut down, outputting diagnostic debugging data into memory. The main flight computer misinterpreted this debugging data as actual flight attitude data, triggering exteme engine steering correction that destroyed the rocket.
This failure shows  the importance of complexity and evolving requirements. Ther Inertial refernce system was directly reused from Ariane 4 without reassessing the system in its enirety or updating its requiremnts for Ariane 5's higher acceleration. Because the system was not properly re validated across its evolving process, reusing legacy code to save development effort resulted in system destruction. Failing to apply software engineering methods in large evolving projects creates long term costs and problems.
Source: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/340875112_Ariane_501_Rocket_Explosion

## Is There Still a Software Crisis?
The 1968 and 1969 NATO Software Engineering Conferences were organised by the NATO Science Commitee's study group on computer science, it was led by a computer scientist name Friedrich L.Bauer. The 1969 Garmisch conference was specifically organised in response to the massive failures and long delays of large scale operating system projects of the era. An example is IBM's OS/360, which showed that managing large programming team required structured organisation rather than just individual talent. 
A version of the software crisis still exists today, it has shifted from a crisis of writing code to a crisis of managing system complexity and legacy dependencies. The 1996 Ariane 501 rocket explosion is an example of this problem because the engineers reused the software from Ariane 4 without re validating it which led to its explosion. Software engineering has definitely matured but still faces some of the same fundamental crisis.
